codex
The paper’s own classification results are deliberately easy at low color counts: Theorem 3.1 gives complete 3-critical lists, Theorem 4.1 gives finite/near-finite 4-critical lists for \(s_1\ge2\), Proposition 5.2 bounds caterpillar colorability, and Proposition 2.3 makes the \((2,2,2)\) case just paths/cycles. Those regimes cannot support the generator.

The remaining viable route is the paper’s exact \(S\)-packing coloring definition in its ordinary-coloring specialization \(S=(1,1,\ldots)\), which Section 1 explicitly identifies. I’m validating the complexity basis and then I’ll stress-test a planted fixed-\(k\) coloring distribution; if its planting is algorithmically visible, I’ll reject rather than ship it.
